protesters are gathering on the streets  of the thai capital  bangkok as lawmakers vote on possible  reforms  to the constitution the rally comes a  day after the most violent clashes  between police and demonstrators  since july police used tear gas and  water cannons to drive backgrounds  dozens were injured including six people  who were hit by gunshots bangkok has  seen months of youth-led  rallies calling for changes to the  constitution and  most controversially reforms to  thailand's monarchy  let's cross over to bangkok where  journalist philip sherbal has been  following this story for us protesters  have returned to the streets today  you've talked to him i understand uh  what are they saying  yeah they really want to um make a point  they were gathering outside parliament  yesterday where  mps have been considering possible  changes to the constitution  and um the the this is one of the core  demands of the protesters  um that those protests yesterday ended  in some fairly nasty clashes um  and um they uh really want to sort of  return to the streets now make clear  that they're not being cowed  and also there is talk that they may  march on the police headquarters the  police are certainly  expecting that and they've um barricaded  themselves in  um using trucks and razor wire to sort  of blockade the gates and warn that  they'll be very decisive action against  protesters if they do try  to make a move there it's not clear if  the protesters will do that or if  they're actually  trying to fool the police and might have  another destination as their ultimate  end because they have been  known to do that in previous weeks as  well  and while we're looking at live pictures  uh of that  protest going on what can you tell us  about the constitutional  constitutional amendments that are  currently being voted on  yes well um so the parliament has been  sitting yesterday and  mps have been and senators have been  voting today on a series of  possible amendments to the constitution  now  the constitution was drafted by the  military under the old junta  and um basically enshrines the sort of  role of the monarchy in key positions in  the in the country and  um indeed of the military-backed elite  and the  uh protesters have been insistent that  if you're gonna have change in thailand  change from the top  then you have to rewrite this  constitution now the versions that are  being voted on today  run the gamut from very minor tweaks to  um  to major changes but it does seem that  the mps and senators it's a complicated  process of voting for different  amendments  are going to back perhaps not  surprisingly because they are the  government majority  some very sort of conservative  amendments that would only essentially  establish a drafting committee to look  at possible changes down the road  the students and the protesters will be  very unhappy with that and that  they told me today if those votes are  confirmed while they're out in the  streets they're going to make that  um central to what they're doing today  in terms of their messaging  and in terms of their um and the  demonstrations so what about the  changes to the monarchy that  demonstrators  want and are they likely to to get it  uh the answer to that is are they they  probably aren't they're facing a huge  uphill challenge  um the the constitution the early  clauses of it this this military-drafted  constitution  do enshrine the um the monarchy's power  um to sort of to govern in key regards  um the  the king also has assumed powers  since he became the monarch in 2016 over  key elements of the budget and over key  military appointments  the students um insist this has to  change that thailand  uh that this is not the constitutional  monarchy that thailand um  is on paper they say in practice um  the power of the royal family is way too  strong but they're up against the royal  family the military that backs them  um and big businesses that sort of  support the  current um the current establishment so  you know they are um they're up against  very strong vested interests and to  achieve change on that front would be a  major breakthrough but  they're determined to show on the  streets that they have the energy and  the  um the momentum to keep this going  journalist philip  sherbal reporting for us from bangkok in  thailand thank you  thank you
 

